  "label": "Disease State Transition Models",
  "related": [
    "genome-aware-variant-aaa5",
    "mechanisms-viral-a1e5",
    "tuning-neuronal-61d2"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "activity-disorders-consciousness-ba5a",
  "summary": "Recent preprints frame protein aggregation, actin depletion, and sequestration noise as state-dependent dynamical transitions.",
  "synthesis": "The three preprints share a dominant thread: system behavior is governed by state-dependent transitions set by resource or binding constraints, rather than by a common model class. The aggregation study uses a phase-plane formulation in which aggregate formation and removal produce cell-level bistability, linking in vitro kinetics to disease onset. The actin study uses an ODE model in which local depletion of a finite monomer pool determines whether branched networks coexist or one is selected, with finite element simulations agreeing. The gene expression study is stochastic: protein sequestration modulates expression noise, with non-cooperative binding yielding gamma distributions of reduced noise. What is new is explicit finite-pool depletion as a selection mechanism and sequestration as a noise-shaping mechanism, each framed dynamically.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-30"
}
